Base.Relics.TheSirenVase_DescŸBlock your ears or bind yourself to the mast, for the Siren Vase sings a song you cannot resist! This stunning Attic red-figured stamnos, painted around 480-470 BC by the anonymous artist known as the Siren Painter, is one of the finest surviving paintings from ancient Greece. Found at Vulci in Etruria, it now rests in a museum, where it continues to enchant visitors thousands of years on.<br><br>The vase depicts one of the most famous scenes in all mythology: Odysseus, bound tightly to the mast of his ship, listens to the song of the Sirens while his crew rows on with wax plugging their ears. The Sirens themselves are shown not as the mermaids of later tradition, but as they were originally imagined - women with the bodies of birds, perched on rocky outcrops above the sea. One dives headlong toward the water, doomed to die now that a mortal has heard their song and survived.<br><br>The Siren Vase captures a moment of tension between temptation and survival, desire and duty. It is a window into the Greek imagination, painted with such life that you can almost hear the music drifting off the clay.Ú
