&Base.Units.Unit_CatherineDeMedici_Descë
Power flows through poison and prophecy when Catherine de Medici takes the throne! This 16th-century Italian noblewoman became Queen of France and the most formidable woman in European politics, ruling through three of her sons as the power behind the throne. Born in Florence to the wealthy Medici banking family, she arrived in France as a teenage bride and transformed into a master of courtly intrigue.<br><br>When her husband Henry II died in a jousting accident, Catherine emerged from the shadows to become the true ruler of France during the violent Wars of Religion. She navigated between Catholic and Huguenot factions with Machiavellian precision, arranging marriages, organizing spectacular court festivals, and when necessary, orchestrating darker deeds. The St. Bartholomew's Day Massacre remains her most controversial legacy, though historians still debate her exact role.<br><br>Catherine brought Italian Renaissance culture to France, patronizing arts and architecture while consulting with Nostradamus and studying poisons in her private laboratory. She bore ten children and used them as political pawns across Europe's chessboard. Though called everything from the 'Black Queen' to the 'Serpent of Florence,' Catherine kept France from fragmenting during its bloodiest period.≠
