'Base.Units.Unit_BartholomewRoberts_Desc∫Put down that rum and mind your manners, for Bartholomew Roberts‚Äîthe infamous 'Black Bart' has arrived! A reluctant recruit who became the scourge of the Atlantic, this Welshman ruled the shipping lanes from the African coast to the Caribbean, answering neither to the Royal Navy nor any colonial governor. Renowned for his staggering success rate, he transformed piracy from chaotic robbery into a chillingly efficient business.<br><br>His legend unfolds during the twilight of the 'Golden Age of Piracy'. In a tragically short career, he swept across the ocean like a disciplined storm, capturing over 400 vessels; more than any of his contemporaries. Yet, Roberts was far more than a mere brute. He was a figure of flamboyant contradictions, known for sailing into battle dressed in rich crimson damask, and for his dramatic final stand against the HMS Swallow, where he died on deck rather than face capture.<br><br>What set Roberts apart was his paradoxical nature. Unlike the drunken, disorderly stereotypes of his trade, he was a teetotaler who preferred tea to grog and loathed swearing. He enforced strict 'articles' aboard his ships, banning gambling, protecting women, and demanding musicians play on Sundays, carving his own path with a strange, puritanical professionalism that cut when weaponized. From 18th-century broadsheets to modern lore, Black Bart endures as the era's most formidable captain: a man whose discipline made him king of the seas.9
