!Base.Units.Unit_HowardCarter_DescµThis British archaeologist and Egyptologist spent decades searching the sands of Egypt in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, driven by an obsession that would change history. When others told him to give up, Howard Carter kept digging.<br><br>On November 26, 1922, Carter made a tiny hole in a sealed doorway in the Valley of the Kings. When asked what he could see by the flickering candlelight, he whispered: 'Wonderful things.' He had discovered the intact tomb of Tutankhamun - the greatest archaeological find of all time. Golden shrines, jeweled treasures, and the famous death mask awaited within.<br><br>Carter spent the next decade meticulously cataloguing over 5,000 artifacts from the tomb, setting new standards for archaeological practice. His discovery sparked 'Tutmania' across the globe and brought Ancient Egypt into the modern imagination. When his patron Lord Carnarvon died months later, newspapers said they were both cursed for disturbing the pharaoh's rest, but Carter dismissed such nonsense and proved them wrong by living another seventeen years.O
