&Base.Units.Unit_CatherineTheGreat_DescﬁThe Winter Palace shimmers with reason and ambition, for Catherine the Great, Empress of All Russia, has arrived! Born Sophie Friederike Auguste in 1729, a minor German princess, she came to the Russian court with no army and no fortune, only a mind that devoured every book it could find. In 1762, she seized the throne, and Russia would never be the same.<br><br>Over thirty-four years as Empress, Catherine lit Russia with the Enlightenment's flame. She corresponded with Voltaire, welcomed Diderot to her court, and offered to publish the great Encyclop√©die in Russia when France moved to ban it. She crafted the Nakaz, a legal code rooted in reason and justice, founded the Smolny Institute, the first state-funded school for women in Europe, and began the art collection that became the Hermitage. When smallpox ravaged Europe, Catherine was the first in Russia to be inoculated, launching a nationwide campaign that saved countless lives.<br><br>Few rulers in history deserved the word 'Great' as much as Catherine. Her reign is remembered as Russia's Golden Age, and her legacy is written in marble and oil paint, in legal codes and school charters, across a vast empire. Yet even the greatest legacies cast long shadows. For the Ukrainian people, Catherine's reign brought the end of centuries of autonomy, the dismantling of their traditional institutions, and a policy of Russification that suppressed their language, a reminder that her enlightened ideals were not extended equally to all.≠
