Base.Relics.NarmerPalette_Desc±Before there were pyramids, before there were pharaohs as we know them, there was a king who struck down his enemies and united a nation. The Narmer Palette, carved over 5,000 years ago, tells that story. On one side, King Narmer wears the white crown of Upper Egypt, smiting a kneeling foe. On the other, he dons the red crown of Lower Egypt, surveying the bodies of his conquered enemies. Two lands become one. A civilization is born.<br><br>This slab of carved greenish siltstone is considered one of humanity's earliest historical documents. It predates formal writing, yet its meaning is unmistakable. The gods watch over Narmer. His enemies are beneath him. Egypt is whole. Everything that followed, from Tutankhamun's treasures to the colossal statues of Ramesses, began here. One king. One victory. One Egypt.n
