 Base.Units.Unit_HelenOfTroy_Desc≥	Beauty becomes a blade when Helen of Troy joins your ranks, the woman whose face launched a thousand ships and burned the topless towers of Ilium! Born from Zeus and Leda in Greek mythology, this Spartan princess was so beautiful that all the kings of Greece swore an oath to protect her marriage. When Paris of Troy spirited her away from King Menelaus, that oath ignited the Trojan War.<br><br>For ten years, Helen lived in Troy as Paris's wife, though ancient sources disagree whether she was victim or willing participant. She walked the walls identifying Greek warriors for Priam and wove tapestries depicting the war fought in her name, while Trojan women whispered behind her back as their husbands died defending her presence. Though Homer called her 'the face that launched a thousand ships,' Helen's story is more complex than mere beauty.<br><br>Helen's legacy transcends the war itself. She became immortal in literature and art, a symbol of dangerous beauty and the devastating power of passion. After Troy's destruction, she returned to Sparta where she and Menelaus lived out their days, though poets gave her a different ending - transformation into a goddess on the Isle of the Blessed.É
