Base.Units.Unit_Odysseus_Desc¨	Steer the ship and hold your nerve, for Odysseus, King of Ithaca, the wiliest hero of all Greece, has joined the war! Described in Homer's epic poems, the 'Iliad' and the 'Odyssey', this legendary warrior and strategist fought at the Siege of Troy and devised the Trojan Horse, the trick that ended the war in a single night.<br><br>But it was the journey home that forged his legend. Cursed to wander for ten years, Odysseus faced impossible trials: he outwitted the Cyclops Polyphemus, resisted the song of the Sirens, and confronted the dread Scylla, a six-headed monster lurking in a fog-shrouded cliff. Warned by Circe that Scylla could not be killed and that Charybdis, the great whirlpool, would swallow his entire ship, Odysseus made an agonising choice: he steered toward the monster, knowing six of his men would die so the rest might live. It was, Homer tells us, the most heart-wrenching moment of all his wanderings.<br><br>Through cunning, endurance, and sheer force of will, Odysseus survived where others perished, proving that the mind can triumph where the sword cannot. The word 'odyssey' itself now means an epic journey: lasting proof that his story has never been forgotten.F
