Base.Units.Unit_Hector_Desc·Steady your shields and hold the line with Hector of Troy, the man who stood between his city and annihilation! When the black ships of Greece darkened Troy's shores, this prince became the backbone of Trojan resistance. Unlike warriors who fought for glory or gold, Hector fought for something greater - the survival of everything he loved.<br><br>For nine years, Hector was Troy's shield and spear. He led from the front, turning back Greek assaults and even driving them to their ships. His duel with Ajax shook the earth, ending only when heralds separated them at nightfall. But Hector was more than a warrior - he was a devoted son to Priam, a loving husband to Andromache, and the moral center of a city under siege.<br><br>His death at Achilles' hands wasn't just the loss of Troy's greatest defender - it was the moment the city's fate was sealed. Yet even in death, Hector achieved something remarkable: he humanized the enemy. When Priam retrieved his son's body, even hard-hearted Achilles wept. Hector's legacy isn't one of conquest but of duty, showing that true heroism lies in defending what matters most.A
