!Base.Relics.TheLewisChessmen_Desc£	Unearthed from the sands of Scotland's Isle of Lewis in 1831, the Lewis Chessmen are among the most famous archaeological discoveries in history. This hoard of 93 gaming pieces, carved from walrus ivory and whale teeth in the 12th century, includes kings, queens, bishops, knights, warders, and pawns, each one unique and bursting with personality. Their origins are likely Scandinavian, with most scholars pointing to Trondheim in Norway as their birthplace, though how they ended up buried on a remote Scottish island remains a mystery.<br><br>The pieces themselves are extraordinary. Seated kings clutch swords across their laps, wide-eyed queens press their hands to their cheeks in worry, and mitred bishops gaze serenely ahead. But the most famous of all are the berserker warders - wild-eyed rooks who gnaw on the tops of their shields in a frenzy of battle rage, capturing the spirit of the Norse sagas in miniature. Christian and pagan influences sit side by side across the set, a snapshot of a world caught between old gods and new.<br><br>The chessmen have inspired novels, films, and countless reproductions, and remain among the most beloved objects in any museum on Earth.?
