Base.Units.Unit_Telemachus_Descî
Step into the sandals of a prince forged by absence with Telemachus, the son of Odysseus and Penelope in Homer's Odyssey. Born on the island of Ithaca just before his father departed for the Trojan War, he grew to manhood in a palace overrun by suitors devouring his family's wealth and pressing his mother for marriage. For twenty years he waited, uncertain whether his father was alive or lost forever.<br><br>Guided by the goddess Athena, who appeared to him in disguise, Telemachus set sail for Pylos and Sparta in search of news about Odysseus. At the courts of Nestor and Menelaus he heard tales of his father's cunning and valor, and learned that Odysseus still lived. The journey transformed him from a hesitant youth into a decisive young leader, one willing to stand against the powerful men who had taken root in his home.<br><br>His defining moment came when Odysseus finally returned to Ithaca disguised as a beggar. Father and son devised a plan together, and when Penelope's archery contest exposed the suitors' weakness, Telemachus fought alongside Odysseus to reclaim their household. His story endures as a testament to patience, loyalty, and the courage it takes to grow into the person you were meant to become, even when the one who should have shown you the way was never there.Œ
