Base.Relics.OdysseusBow_DescÀNo one else could string it. No one else could draw it. Odysseus' bow is one of the most legendary weapons in all of literature, a symbol of strength, cunning, and rightful authority from Homer's 'Odyssey'. This great composite bow was a gift from Iphitus, son of the famed archer Eurytus, given in friendship long before the Trojan War.<br><br>For twenty years, while Odysseus fought at Troy and wandered the wine-dark seas, the bow lay untouched in his palace at Ithaca. When suitors swarmed his home, vying for the hand of his faithful wife Penelope, she devised a contest: whoever could string the bow and fire an arrow through the rings of twelve axes would win her hand. One by one, the suitors tried. One by one, they failed. Then Odysseus, disguised as a beggar, took up the bow. With ease, he strung it, and the string sang like a swallow's cry.<br><br>What followed was swift and merciless. Arrow after arrow found its mark as Odysseus reclaimed his home and his name. The bow is more than a weapon - it is a test of identity, a proof that some things can only belong to their true master.D
